{
  "id": "nomen-verb-verbindungen",
  "name": "Verb + noun collocations",
  "level": "B2",
  "subtitle": "Some verbs and nouns belong together: make a decision, take a risk, pay attention. The verb is fixed, and a different verb sounds wrong. Learn the pair as one phrase.",
  "briefMarks": [
    "make a decision",
    "take a risk",
    "pay attention"
  ],
  "source": "icanspeak English original English text (grammar-b2 run, 2026-10-29)",
  "concept": {
    "flow": [
      {
        "text": "fixed verb",
        "variant": "verb"
      },
      {
        "text": "+"
      },
      {
        "text": "noun",
        "variant": "prep"
      },
      {
        "text": "= one phrase"
      }
    ],
    "exampleHtml": "She <span class=\"vp-verb\">made</span> a <span class=\"vp-prep\">decision</span>. — Please <span class=\"vp-verb\">pay</span> <span class=\"vp-prep\">attention</span>. — We <span class=\"vp-verb\">took</span> a <span class=\"vp-prep\">break</span>.",
    "qa": [
      {
        "html": "A <strong>collocation</strong> is a pair of words that native speakers always use together. In <em>make a decision</em> you cannot change the verb: <em>do a decision</em> sounds wrong, even if people understand you. Learn the verb and the noun as <strong>one phrase</strong>.",
        "tag": "Fixed pairs"
      },
      {
        "html": "Seven verbs make most of these phrases: <strong>make</strong> a decision / an effort / progress · <strong>take</strong> a risk / a break / advantage of · <strong>pay</strong> attention / a visit / a compliment · <strong>have</strong> a look / a go · <strong>give</strong> advice / a talk · <strong>keep</strong> a promise · <strong>break</strong> the law / a promise.",
        "tag": "The key verbs"
      },
      {
        "html": "The verb changes its form as usual (<em>made, took, paid, gave, kept, broke</em>). Watch the article. Some nouns are uncountable and take no <em>a</em>: <em>pay attention, give advice, make progress</em>. Others need one: <em>take a break, make an effort, have a look</em>.",
        "tag": "Tense and article"
      },
      {
        "html": "Typical mistakes: ✗ <em>We must do a decision.</em> → ✓ <em>We must make a decision.</em> · ✗ <em>Let's make a break.</em> → ✓ <em>Let's take a break.</em> · ✗ <em>Can you give me an advice?</em> → ✓ <em>Can you give me some advice?</em> (advice has no <em>a</em> and no plural)",
        "tag": "Typical mistakes"
      }
    ],
    "noteHtml": "Collocations are normal in every kind of English, from chats to reports, so it pays to learn them. Make and do have their own topic: <a href=\"/make-or-do\">make or do</a>. British English often says <em>have a look</em>, <em>have a break</em> and <em>have a go</em>; <em>take a look</em> and <em>take a break</em> are also common, especially in American English. In British English you can say <em>take a decision</em>, but <em>make a decision</em> is safe everywhere. Some nouns have two verbs with different meanings: <em>give a talk</em> (a lecture) and <em>have a talk</em> (a chat). For verbs with fixed prepositions, see <a href=\"/verbs-prepositions\">Verbs + prepositions</a>.",
    "reference": {
      "showLabel": "Show list ▼",
      "hideLabel": "Hide list ▲",
